// Non-blocking I/O and readiness polling -- serving MANY connections from one thread. A program
// asks `poll` which of its sockets can be acted on right now, then advances each one a step and
// comes back. There is no callback and no scheduler: the loop is the program's own, which is what
// lets it keep per-connection state in an ordinary Map.
//
// OPT-IN: reach these wrappers AND the raw natives via `use std::poll::*`. Builds on std::net --
// a listener or connection is created there (blocking) and taken over here.
//
// THE THREE OUTCOMES. On a non-blocking socket, "nothing right now" is neither a value nor an
// error, and it is NOT the same as "the peer closed". `accept` and `recv` therefore answer with an
// enum, not with an empty result -- confusing the two is what makes a server either drop live
// connections or keep dead ones forever.
//
// SENDING IS PARTIAL. `send` returns how many bytes the kernel ACCEPTED, which may be fewer than
// offered, or zero. There is deliberately no send-all: waiting for the rest is exactly the blocking
// this module exists to avoid. The caller keeps the unsent tail and retries when `poll` reports the
// socket writable.
//
// AN ACTOR CAN WAIT ON BOTH. `selectIo` below covers a program's sockets AND an actor's inboxes in
// one wait, which is what lets a connection actor read its own socket instead of handing the reading
// to the runtime (`c.activate` in std::net). Neither other wait can: `select` parks on a condition
// variable, which no socket can wake, and `poll` watches only sockets. Which of the two to use is a
// real choice, not a formality -- see the note on `selectIo`.
//
// LIMITS (deliberate): ONE core -- this is concurrency, not parallelism; a CPU-heavy handler blocks
// every connection, and so does a garbage-collection pause; `connect` stays BLOCKING (std::net's
// has its own timeout), so there is no non-blocking client dial; plaintext only, as in std::net.
use std::actor::*
use std::net::*

// Readiness flags, as returned by `poll` and passed to it as interest. They are a packed Int, not
// an enum, because a socket can be several of these at once -- which is the one thing an enum
// cannot say. Test with `(flags & READABLE) != 0`.
pub const READABLE: Int = 1     // data can be read, or a connection is waiting to be accepted
pub const WRITABLE: Int = 2     // a send has room in the kernel buffer
pub const CLOSED:   Int = 4     // hangup or error; the socket is done either way

// A listening socket in non-blocking mode. `fd` is the descriptor -- the key to use when the
// program keeps its own per-socket state, and what goes into the vector handed to `poll`.
pub struct NbListener { fd: Int }

// An accepted or taken-over connection in non-blocking mode.
pub struct NbConn { fd: Int }

// The outcome of a non-blocking accept: either a connection or nobody waiting.
pub enum Accepted { Conn(NbConn), WouldBlock }

// The outcome of a non-blocking read. `Closed` is the peer having shut down -- final, the socket
// will never produce data again. `WouldBlock` is the opposite: nothing has arrived YET.
pub enum Received { Data(Bytes), WouldBlock, Closed }

// --- taking a std::net socket over ---

// Switch a listener into non-blocking mode. The blocking `l` must not be used afterwards; its
// `accept` would no longer wait.
pub fn nonBlocking(l: TcpListener) -> Result[NbListener, String] {
  rawSetNonBlocking(l.fd, true)?
  Ok(NbListener { fd: l.fd })
}

// Switch a connection into non-blocking mode -- the client side of a loop, since `connect` itself
// stays blocking.
pub fn nonBlockingConn(c: TcpConn) -> Result[NbConn, String] {
  rawSetNonBlocking(c.fd, true)?
  Ok(NbConn { fd: c.fd })
}

// --- the readiness scan ---

// Wait until at least one socket is ready or `timeoutMs` expires (0 = return at once, a negative
// value = wait indefinitely). `interest` says per socket what to watch for, as an OR of READABLE
// and WRITABLE; asking for WRITABLE with nothing to send makes `poll` return immediately every
// time, which turns the loop into a spin.
//
// WATCHING NOTHING IS ALLOWED and still waits: an empty `fds`, or an interest set that is all
// zeros, sleeps out the timeout and then reports nothing ready -- so a loop round with no
// outstanding work costs no CPU, and `poll(vec(), vec(), ms)` is a plain sleep. The one refusal is
// a NEGATIVE timeout with no interest at all: nothing could ever end that wait, so it is an Err
// rather than a program that hangs with no output.
//
// The result is INDEX-PARALLEL to `fds`: element i holds the flags for fds[i], 0 when nothing
// happened. Both vectors must have the same length.
pub fn poll(fds: Vec[Int], interest: Vec[Int], timeoutMs: Int) -> Result[Array[Int], String] {
  rawPoll(fds, interest, timeoutMs)
}

// Wait until one of `boxes` has something to read, or one of `fds` is ready, or `timeoutMs` expires
// (0 = look and return at once, a negative value = wait indefinitely). `boxes` holds `inbox.ref()`
// tokens, exactly as `select` takes them; `fds` and `interest` are `poll`'s.
//
// The result is INDEX-PARALLEL to `boxes` FOLLOWED BY `fds`, so it is len(boxes) + len(fds) long: the
// first len(boxes) entries are 1 when that inbox has something (a message, a crash report, or the
// actor's Stop) and 0 otherwise, and the rest are `poll`'s flag words. Test them in the order that
// matters to you -- unlike `select`, which answers ONE index and makes the lowest one a priority, this
// reports everything that is ready and leaves the ranking to you.
//
// It reports readiness and takes nothing out, so the `receive()` or `recv()` that follows cannot wait.
// As in `poll`, watching nothing still waits; a negative timeout with no inbox AND no interest is an
// Err, since nothing could end that wait.
//
// WHEN NOT TO USE IT. Waiting on a socket costs a different kind of wait underneath, and it does not
// spin first the way an inbox-only wait does. An actor whose partner answers within a few microseconds
// -- a tight request/reply between two actors -- is measurably WORSE off here than in `select`, while an
// actor that waits tens of microseconds or more pays little. So reach for this when an actor really has
// to watch a socket and its inbox together; keep `select`, or std::net's `c.activate`, otherwise.
// `activate` also remains the better answer when you want line framing, back-pressure through an
// inbox's capacity, an activated LISTENER, or a deadline that closes a client that stopped reading.
//
// On Windows at most 63 sockets may be watched in one call.
pub fn selectIo(boxes: Vec[InboxRef], fds: Vec[Int], interest: Vec[Int],
                timeoutMs: Int) -> Result[Array[Int], String] {
  rawSelectIo(boxes, fds, interest, timeoutMs)
}

// --- a listener's own API ---

impl NbListener {
  // Take the next waiting connection, or report that none is waiting. Never blocks. The returned
  // connection is already non-blocking.
  fn accept(self) -> Result[Accepted, String] {
    let fd = rawAcceptNb(self.fd)?
    if fd < 0 { Ok(Accepted::WouldBlock) } else { Ok(Accepted::Conn(NbConn { fd: fd })) }
  }

  // Stop listening.
  fn close(self) -> Result[(), String] { tcpClose(self.fd) }
}

// --- a connection's own API ---

impl NbConn {
  // Read up to `n` bytes without waiting. `Data` carries at least one byte; `Closed` means the peer
  // shut down; `WouldBlock` means nothing has arrived yet.
  fn recv(self, n: Int) -> Result[Received, String] {
    let got = rawRecvNb(self.fd, n)?
    if len(got) == 0 {
      Ok(Received::WouldBlock)
    } else {
      let b = got[0]
      if len(b) == 0 { Ok(Received::Closed) } else { Ok(Received::Data(b)) }
    }
  }

  // Offer `data` to the kernel and report how many bytes it took -- possibly fewer than offered,
  // possibly none. Retry the remainder when `poll` next reports the socket writable.
  fn send(self, data: Bytes) -> Result[Int, String] { rawSendNb(self.fd, data) }

  // Offer a string's bytes; same partial-write rule as `send`.
  fn sendStr(self, s: String) -> Result[Int, String] { rawSendNb(self.fd, toBytes(s)) }

  // Close the connection and free its descriptor.
  fn close(self) -> Result[(), String] { tcpClose(self.fd) }
}
